# ZenRay Assets V2 (2 October 2026)

Each project has one stable id, one cloud folder and one canonical feedback file. Local directories remain where the creator works. (2 October 2026)

## Daily protocol (2 October 2026)

1. Open Assets and choose Relier un projet. Enter a name to create a TMP workshop and a Memory project automatically. (2 October 2026)
2. Work inside TMP without arranging files for the cloud. Add scoped Houdini and Unreal sources when they exist. (2 October 2026)
3. Files, chronology, prompts and source locations use the same project id. Save all files or a selection; unchanged files are skipped. (2 October 2026)
4. Load into a separate workspace. SHA256 and byte length independently verify restored originals; the source scene remains intact. (2 October 2026)
5. Download a single original or several originals in a ZIP that preserves source folders. Never substitute a transcoded stream for the original. (2 October 2026)

## Sources and Unreal exception (2 October 2026)

Cloud layout is `pages/ZenRayAssets/<project-id>/sources/<namespace>/<source-id>/<relative-path>`. Namespaces are tmp, memory, houdini and unreal. (2 October 2026)

ZenRayShards is a shared host project. Config, Plugins and the uproject belong to `zenray-shards-core` once. Ceramic Tiles and Louisiana Bayou link to this host and include only their own Content paths and external world-partition actors. Engine caches, Intermediate and generated temporary files are excluded. (2 October 2026)

Selected Unreal content is not proof of a standalone runnable game: dependency closure must be verified in Unreal before declaring the project portable. (2 October 2026)

## Memory and capture (2 October 2026)

The canonical feedback is `Memory1/Projects/<id>/FEEDBACK.md`. Existing historical files remain intact and link to it. The writer locks, appends, syncs and deduplicates stable event ids. The feedback skill delegates to feedback-stack. (2 October 2026)

The local bridge reads bound Codex or Claude transcripts incrementally, captures human prompts and successful final results, and routes a uniquely scoped working directory. Ambiguous sessions require an explicit project binding; they are never guessed. (2 October 2026)

The chronology reads the existing global PROGRESS.md alongside project feedback and transfer events. It does not create a second global journal. (2 October 2026)

## Runtime and proof (2 October 2026)

Source configuration lives in scripts/AssetsConfig.json. Runtime inventory, hashes, subscriptions, transcript offsets and append-only events live under Library/Application Support/ZenRayAssets. Browser code never imports local filesystem code. (2 October 2026)

Use `node scripts/Assets/Daemon.mjs install` to enable the bridge at login and `node scripts/Assets/Daemon.mjs status` to inspect it. Credentials are read from the existing gh login and remain in process memory. (2 October 2026)

The existing ZenRayCloud watcher implementation supplies filesystem observation, debounce, reconnect and periodic sweep. The bridge compiles that implementation locally rather than duplicating its logic. (2 October 2026)

Unload currently produces a protected plan and does not delete source files. It needs a verified archive and independent successful restore before a future recoverable release operation can proceed. (2 October 2026)

Design controls modify CSS root tokens, persist locally and copy their values for a prompt. The controls are excluded from production builds. (2 October 2026)

Verification: `npm exec vitest run -- scripts/AssetsService.test.js src/lib/assetsWorkspaceViewModel.test.js src/lib/assetsProjects.test.js src/lib/bulkZip.test.js`. Append-only concurrency verification lives in the canonical feedback-stack Tests/TestFeedback.py. (2 October 2026)

## Every new TMP file follows the same route (2 October 2026)

The boundary is the project workshop, created automatically from its stable project id. A creator supplies the project name once, then saves any files inside that workshop, in any subdirectory. No per-file registration or local category sorting is required. (2 October 2026)

A filesystem event marks the project dirty. The watcher waits for edits to settle, inventories regular files, excludes temporary writes and records a source id plus a relative path. A stable snapshot is hashed with SHA256 before upload. (2 October 2026)

The cloud manifest is recorded only after the archive engine verifies the file. An incremental search-index update makes it discoverable. A project snapshot updates the dashboard. Errors preserve the source and retry; local deletions never silently delete cloud history. (2 October 2026)

A queue serializes cloud writes. The initial bulk backup can delay another project; the dashboard must show queued or running rather than claiming a saved file. Daily changes reuse prior fingerprints and do not re-upload unchanged originals. (2 October 2026)

An arbitrary file outside registered sources has no inferred project. It must first be attached through a source mapping. Automatic capture never assigns a file to a project merely because it is the latest project opened. (2 October 2026)

Restart performs a fresh inventory of watched projects. Periodic sweeps catch filesystem notifications missed while the machine sleeps. Original names, relative paths and fingerprints allow exact reconstruction. (2 October 2026)

Verified manifests enter a persistent pending-index list before publication. Each batch drains this list; a crash leaves it available for retry without transferring original bytes again. Dashboard snapshots publish at most once per minute during a bulk job, then once at completion. (2 October 2026)

Restoring exact bytes does not validate absolute Houdini references or Unreal dependencies. A runnable DCC project needs its dependency paths checked against the loaded workspace before claiming portability. (2 October 2026)

## Managed release and immutable recovery (2 October 2026)

The earlier unload-plan limitation is superseded for the active workspace produced by LOAD. Production source folders remain untouched. Unload refuses modified, extra, missing and symbolic files, then proves cloud recovery again before removing managed copies. A complete workspace selection is required. (2 October 2026)

Release records persist before removal and preserve exact backup fingerprints. Recovery selects content by SHA256, including retained older versions. Restart reconciles interrupted releases without deleting data. Reported allocated file blocks are distinct from whole-volume free-space fluctuations. (2 October 2026)

Run `node scripts/Assets/VerifyLifecycle.mjs` for the real cloud witness cycle. Its isolated workspace keeps the source intact and writes Evidence.json. The UI exposes release and recovery as jobs with existing progress notifications. (2 October 2026)

## Bounded automatic passes (2 October 2026)

Automatic backups yield after `storage.autoBatchLimit` verified batches, then append their remaining work to the queue. Queued actions and other projects can progress between passes. A single large file still defines the minimum non-preemptible transfer. Manual full archives remain full jobs. (2 October 2026)

Interactive jobs precede waiting automatic passes, while the active writer finishes its verified batch. Compact rows keep source and cloud state visible, and waiting actions are labelled as queued rather than actively verifying. (2 October 2026)

## Bounded local text previews (2 October 2026)

Registered Markdown, JSON, logs and scripts can be read as inert text in the local preview. `src/lib/projectPreview.js` owns allowed extensions and the byte limit. Unknown paths, symlinks, changing files and binary content are refused; truncation is explicit and never changes the source. (2 October 2026)

## Independent local lifecycle lane (2 October 2026)

LOAD, UNLOAD and recovery serialize per project with a dedicated lifecycle lock while the global cloud writer continues uploads. Per-project JSON saves are ordered to retain both sets of evidence. Live sync states derive from active jobs. Operation indicators follow their project when the user changes the active view. (2 October 2026)

